1
00:00:18,819 --> 00:00:23,802
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I was wanting to talk about emotions.

2
00:00:24,702 --> 00:00:31,286
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, are emotions good or bad?

3
00:00:31,306 --> 00:00:34,868
[SPEAKER_01]: Are they... is there something wrong with them?

4
00:00:35,729 --> 00:00:41,432
[SPEAKER_01]: Should they be cultivated or should they be avoided?

5
00:00:41,452 --> 00:00:42,733
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, how can you cultivate?

6
00:00:42,773 --> 00:00:43,413
How can you avoid it?

7
00:00:46,086 --> 00:00:59,932
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you could give in to your emotions, you know, every little emotion you feel, you could express it and act out of it and, you know, just run with the kind of thing.

8
00:00:59,952 --> 00:01:06,094
[SPEAKER_01]: You feel a sense of love, so you start hugging everyone, telling how much, telling everyone how much you love them.

9
00:01:06,674 --> 00:01:07,915
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

10
00:01:07,955 --> 00:01:11,977
[SPEAKER_01]: You get angry, just get angry, you know, punch, punch someone in the face.

11
00:01:12,037 --> 00:01:12,997
[SPEAKER_01]: Hey, enjoy.

12
00:01:16,340 --> 00:01:20,223
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I mean, I asked that question, but I know the answer.

13
00:01:20,243 --> 00:01:22,285
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, are emotions good or bad?

14
00:01:22,365 --> 00:01:27,689
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, some emotions, people will say, are good and other emotions are bad.

15
00:01:28,749 --> 00:01:33,953
[SPEAKER_01]: But are emotions, the world of emotions, is it a good thing or a bad thing?

16
00:01:34,774 --> 00:01:35,234
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

17
00:01:35,314 --> 00:01:38,137
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have emotions that are good and emotions that are bad.

18
00:01:39,157 --> 00:01:41,980
[SPEAKER_00]: But being liable to emotions, that's not good.

19
00:01:42,702 --> 00:01:45,883
[SPEAKER_00]: Fundamentally, existentially, that is a problem.

20
00:01:47,423 --> 00:01:50,963
[SPEAKER_00]: So, liability to emotions is an issue.

21
00:01:51,744 --> 00:01:54,104
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, well, there you go.

22
00:01:54,364 --> 00:02:02,306
[SPEAKER_00]: You are liable to these waves that are not in your control, that are sometimes good and sometimes bad.

23
00:02:03,386 --> 00:02:06,066
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, either way, they have to be endured.

24
00:02:06,566 --> 00:02:09,887
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, either way, you are under the pressure of them.

25
00:02:10,940 --> 00:02:11,861
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not good.

26
00:02:12,561 --> 00:02:18,166
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not to say, also, all emotions, in terms of the content, are bad.

27
00:02:18,426 --> 00:02:18,587
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

28
00:02:19,027 --> 00:02:23,491
[SPEAKER_00]: There are good emotions, nice emotions, and bad emotions, like unwholesome emotions.

29
00:02:24,312 --> 00:02:27,014
[SPEAKER_00]: But, fundamentally, the nature of emotion is a problem.

30
00:02:27,614 --> 00:02:31,598
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, well, it puts you in that perilous position.

31
00:02:36,277 --> 00:02:41,641
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is being subjected to, you know, feelings.

32
00:02:42,561 --> 00:02:42,921
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

33
00:02:42,961 --> 00:02:44,883
[SPEAKER_00]: That's fundamentally what the problem is.

34
00:02:45,223 --> 00:02:59,292
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, people tend to not think about the feelings or emotions when those are good or when those are not too threatening or not too... not reminding, you know, not dangerous and so on.

35
00:03:00,152 --> 00:03:01,313
[SPEAKER_00]: But those things change.

36
00:03:03,054 --> 00:03:05,436
[SPEAKER_00]: You can get some feelings that will be revealing the

37
00:03:06,843 --> 00:03:10,144
[SPEAKER_00]: The danger, the unpleasantness, and that you don't know the escape from it.

38
00:03:10,984 --> 00:03:13,725
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, and then you realize, ah, feeling is the problem.

39
00:03:14,385 --> 00:03:28,430
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't be in a position of, kind of, pinned down by an unpleasant feeling of something that happened, that you don't know how to escape from, if you haven't been cultivating gratuitous ownership

40
00:03:29,830 --> 00:03:33,691
[SPEAKER_00]: Appropriation and carelessness in regard to the feeling per se.

41
00:03:34,311 --> 00:03:48,175
[SPEAKER_00]: And that unwholesome type of cultivation happens basically through just, you know, accepting the pleasant feeling, taking it for granted, and assuming that you can always outrun the unpleasant feeling.

42
00:03:48,615 --> 00:03:56,337
[SPEAKER_00]: But you don't realize that either way, you're deeply bound with a feeling which is not in your control.

43
00:03:57,718 --> 00:04:02,341
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like that simile I gave before, I think, I don't know if you recorded it, or maybe in just some of the talks.

44
00:04:02,982 --> 00:04:10,227
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, like, you basically, you find yourself, like, riding a horse that you can't get off.

45
00:04:10,968 --> 00:04:16,792
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't come down from it, and it doesn't listen to you, really.

46
00:04:18,293 --> 00:04:26,940
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it goes where it wants to go, and when those things that it wants to see are nice and agreeable, you enjoy the ride,

47
00:04:27,992 --> 00:04:49,118
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, sometimes, something else takes its fancy, and it goes wild, and runs to the sort of, you know, thorny bushes, and cliffs, and rough stones, and you fall off, and then you're dragged, and then you realize, okay, so riding a horse is a dangerous experience, because I'm not in the control of a horse.

48
00:04:51,518 --> 00:04:54,139
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I can pretend everything is fine, when the horse is sort of

49
00:04:54,877 --> 00:05:08,625
[SPEAKER_00]: Just, you know, taking a slow stride along the beach or something, when it's very pleasant and easy, but then when it's galloping through some wild woodland, and I'm getting cut up and beaten, oh, that's a problem.

50
00:05:08,745 --> 00:05:09,866
[SPEAKER_00]: But you don't want to wait for that.

51
00:05:09,886 --> 00:05:16,310
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to start training the horse before it gets into the, you know, areas and positions that will harm you.

52
00:05:17,352 --> 00:05:20,013
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's the same with your mind and same with the feelings.

53
00:05:20,213 --> 00:05:33,877
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're only... if you're enjoying the presence of your mind and your thoughts and the life when feelings are good, you are making yourself liable to then equally suffer when those feelings change.

54
00:05:34,897 --> 00:05:35,357
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's it.

55
00:05:35,397 --> 00:05:37,578
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to experience bad feelings, by the way.

56
00:05:38,898 --> 00:05:43,519
[SPEAKER_00]: Just not having enough of the good feelings that you had is already felt badly.

57
00:05:44,199 --> 00:05:46,420
[SPEAKER_00]: It's already felt unpleasantly.

58
00:05:47,507 --> 00:05:55,653
[SPEAKER_00]: So, because of your dependence on good emotions and good feelings and good moods, you become even more sensitive, more liable to the bad ones.

59
00:05:56,393 --> 00:05:59,075
[SPEAKER_00]: Because what is bad is relative, from that point of view.

60
00:05:59,956 --> 00:06:02,538
[SPEAKER_00]: Not having enough of a pleasant emotion is unpleasant.

61
00:06:03,799 --> 00:06:09,463
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you were not so dependent on the unpleasant emotions... sorry, if you were not so dependent on the pleasant emotions,

62
00:06:11,047 --> 00:06:18,669
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't be so quick, basically, to fall into that resistance of the unpleasant ones, because the mind wouldn't move, because the mind is not that dependent.

63
00:06:20,570 --> 00:06:21,350
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

64
00:06:21,410 --> 00:06:23,910
[SPEAKER_00]: It's kind of dependence on emotions and feelings.

65
00:06:24,050 --> 00:06:29,772
[SPEAKER_00]: Not that you can't... not that you can stop them, but you can certainly not appropriate them.

66
00:06:30,052 --> 00:06:36,173
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Buddha would say, not welcome them, not delight in them, not... not lose perspective in regard to them.

67
00:06:36,353 --> 00:06:37,874
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's... that's what you want to do.

68
00:06:38,783 --> 00:06:46,788
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to be creating a broader context in regard to whatever you're feeling here and now that's enduring.

69
00:06:48,209 --> 00:06:54,133
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're not trying to get rid of it, and you're certainly not trying to indulge it, and you're certainly not trying to explain it and psychologize it.

70
00:06:54,753 --> 00:06:57,355
[SPEAKER_00]: All you're trying to do is remain unmoved by it.

71
00:06:59,083 --> 00:07:13,628
[SPEAKER_00]: And you do so by developing the full understanding of that context, that, you know, that fundamentally they're unownable, they come on their own, like the waves, and that you are the one who chooses to jump into them and then get all wet and soaked and so on.

72
00:07:17,650 --> 00:07:21,031
[SPEAKER_01]: So... so then... so someone might say, well,

73
00:07:23,796 --> 00:07:26,077
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, I love love.

74
00:07:26,437 --> 00:07:27,457
[SPEAKER_01]: I love happiness.

75
00:07:28,277 --> 00:07:37,359
[SPEAKER_01]: I love excitement and that passionate drive, adventure and all that, those exciting emotions.

76
00:07:38,179 --> 00:07:49,962
[SPEAKER_01]: And then they strive just to have that, you know, and focus just on that part.

77
00:07:51,919 --> 00:07:57,061
[SPEAKER_01]: So, is there a way just to have those pleasant feelings?

78
00:07:57,962 --> 00:08:06,725
[SPEAKER_01]: Is focusing on just that possibility of pleasant emotions a good thing?

79
00:08:07,085 --> 00:08:10,547
[SPEAKER_01]: So, is it possible just to have pleasant emotions?

80
00:08:12,448 --> 00:08:15,509
[SPEAKER_01]: First of all, is it possible just to have that?

81
00:08:17,370 --> 00:08:22,393
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the whole thing is, if somebody wants to focus on that, yeah, that's fine.

82
00:08:23,193 --> 00:08:29,617
[SPEAKER_00]: They will experience that degree of happiness, that degree of sensual joy, to that extent, for that amount of time.

83
00:08:32,439 --> 00:08:36,601
[SPEAKER_00]: But then... And if that's their choice, yeah, that's fine.

84
00:08:36,681 --> 00:08:39,503
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing, like, morally wrong with it.

85
00:08:40,443 --> 00:08:43,325
[SPEAKER_00]: Unless, of course, their actions are harming others, and so on.

86
00:08:44,885 --> 00:09:08,581
[SPEAKER_00]: But, universally speaking, from the point of view of a human experience that is liable to certain things, that same person, if they were to have some wisdom, or if they're interested in developing wisdom, should ask themselves, okay, well, what are the prerequisites for me being able to focus only on pleasant experiences, chase them, get as much as I want, enjoy the life, enjoy sensuality?

87
00:09:08,661 --> 00:09:10,722
[SPEAKER_00]: What are the necessary conditions for that?

88
00:09:11,382 --> 00:09:13,484
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, being alive is the first condition.

89
00:09:14,519 --> 00:09:16,300
[SPEAKER_00]: Being healthy is the second condition.

90
00:09:16,821 --> 00:09:20,804
[SPEAKER_00]: Having functioning senses is the third condition.

91
00:09:21,665 --> 00:09:27,029
[SPEAKER_00]: So, pretty much, you know, having strength and zeal is the condition.

92
00:09:27,169 --> 00:09:37,257
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, am I in control of those conditions that are necessary for me to be able to chase the happiness and enjoy it?

93
00:09:38,279 --> 00:09:42,962
[SPEAKER_00]: And that thought will be enough to, you know, reveal the anxiety underneath.

94
00:09:43,042 --> 00:09:48,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Whereby, sure, you can chase the pleasures, if you want, by ignoring the nature of your situation.

95
00:09:49,146 --> 00:09:56,732
[SPEAKER_00]: That you are not in control of that horse, that is liable to sickness, aging, and death, and it will take you, basically, when it goes.

96
00:09:57,807 --> 00:10:10,971
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your happiness, that you're getting on account of that basis, is very limited, very perilous, and very intoxicating, in a sense of, uh, it makes you forget the true nature of a situation.

97
00:10:11,451 --> 00:10:15,433
[SPEAKER_00]: That you could die at any given moment, that you could get sick at any given moment, that your senses will fail.

98
00:10:15,773 --> 00:10:20,654
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, can you enjoy the beautiful sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, if you can't see, hear, smell, taste, or touch?

99
00:10:22,435 --> 00:10:22,915
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

100
00:10:24,523 --> 00:10:28,926
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is like what... that is... that is the kind of the most frightening idea a person would have.

101
00:10:29,066 --> 00:10:32,468
[SPEAKER_00]: Imagine you... you get into that... how is it called medically?

102
00:10:32,508 --> 00:10:38,612
[SPEAKER_00]: Like a locked-in syndrome, you know, where basically you are alive inside, but your senses, everything is just shut out.

103
00:10:39,532 --> 00:10:44,855
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have no perception of the external world, yet you are aware of yourself.

104
00:10:44,895 --> 00:10:47,057
[SPEAKER_00]: That is basically a situation at any given time.

105
00:10:48,085 --> 00:10:50,066
[SPEAKER_00]: That is what existence is.

106
00:10:50,086 --> 00:10:59,888
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just that these, eh... external sense objects, you know, being inducive of lust, and you taking them for granted, and seeking them further outside, makes you basically forget that.

107
00:11:00,068 --> 00:11:04,730
[SPEAKER_00]: Makes you forget your current situation, that you're always fully enclosed within yourself, and there is no way out.

108
00:11:05,210 --> 00:11:09,611
[SPEAKER_00]: Certainly not in that... external sense that you would be, eh... seeking.

109
00:11:10,351 --> 00:11:12,952
[SPEAKER_01]: So, from that universal point of view,

110
00:11:13,434 --> 00:11:24,963
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, it's not good, then, to be blind like that and just intoxicated and infatuated with the current pleasures and happiness and emotions you're feeling, because it masks the real nature of your situation.

111
00:11:25,844 --> 00:11:29,526
[SPEAKER_00]: But, from your individual point of view, if that's what you want to do, yeah, that's fine.

112
00:11:30,027 --> 00:11:34,070
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll get the results and you'll pay the price, one way or the other.

113
00:11:36,862 --> 00:11:46,545
[SPEAKER_01]: And also, as you say, you know, you are not in control of your senses, your body, you know, you get diseases and so on, or your wealth and so on.

114
00:11:46,825 --> 00:11:53,047
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, the external things that you are dependent upon, say, for example, your children,

115
00:11:53,767 --> 00:12:20,785
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, so you... you love seeing, hearing, experiencing your children, you know, watching them grow up, and it's... and it's such a wonderful experience, the birth, and the way they start learning about things, and it's... they're so cute, and it's fascinating, and you're just overflowing with joy and love, and then they get cancer, or someone hits them, you know, have an accident on the road, hits them with a car, and now

116
00:12:21,805 --> 00:12:47,320
[SPEAKER_01]: You're so dependent on that thing, out there, so far away from your your body, which you are not even in control of, let alone another person's body, and you've spilled out so far, so emotionally dependent, when that thing changes in a horrible way, or, you know, just in a simple way, that's disagreeable to you in some, even a small way, you're completely shattered.

117
00:12:48,321 --> 00:12:49,061
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, your life is

118
00:12:52,403 --> 00:13:03,473
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, because you've been riding really, really high up, you've been high up, and now that thing came crashing and you didn't have time to come down safely.

119
00:13:03,574 --> 00:13:10,660
[SPEAKER_00]: There's that dependence on family and children and possessions and wealth and things that are even more removed out of your control.

120
00:13:14,304 --> 00:13:14,564
So...

121
00:13:16,257 --> 00:13:37,781
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, you know, if you're speaking to a person who is dependent on those external things, very emotionally dependent, and, you know, it is a wonderful thing to love your children, to be kind to them, you know, but there is that danger there.

122
00:13:39,221 --> 00:13:39,521
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

123
00:13:40,361 --> 00:13:41,202
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

124
00:13:42,002 --> 00:13:43,022
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the reality of it.

125
00:13:45,242 --> 00:13:53,467
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, people will often then kind of jump into conclusion that, ah, so it's dangerous, so you're saying it's bad to love children.

126
00:13:54,588 --> 00:13:56,549
[SPEAKER_00]: It's, as I said, it's not a moral question.

127
00:13:56,849 --> 00:14:04,734
[SPEAKER_00]: It's from the universal point of view of your situation, what is basically, eh... skillful, what is unskillful.

128
00:14:05,795 --> 00:14:10,878
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's unskillful to live on top of a tree that somebody is cutting and you refuse to come down.

129
00:14:12,559 --> 00:14:14,881
[SPEAKER_00]: That is unskillful, but you're still free to do so.

130
00:14:16,558 --> 00:14:18,980
[SPEAKER_00]: But you will be responsible for everything that happens to you.

131
00:14:22,043 --> 00:14:26,188
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, the choice is on the person.

132
00:14:26,388 --> 00:14:40,422
[SPEAKER_00]: And if, you know, if they don't absolutely know any other way to live, any form of withdrawal of sense restraint, well, the most you can do is encourage them at least to be virtuous within that perilous situation.

133
00:14:41,567 --> 00:14:58,259
[SPEAKER_00]: But if a person does want to practice, and is familiar with the dhamma, and is in that position of depending on external circumstances to a great extent, well then, yeah, you might just want to remind them of the implicit peril and the sense of urgency that's right there, visible to them.

134
00:15:01,311 --> 00:15:16,500
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's that having a perspective on that thing that you love, you know, having a perspective that, yes, things could, you know, you could experience good things, you know, pleasant feelings, so to speak, but you can equally experience painful feelings.

135
00:15:17,040 --> 00:15:23,448
[SPEAKER_01]: Things can be going quite well for you, and then all of a sudden, you know, you get a disease, there's an accident, there's a murder.

136
00:15:24,008 --> 00:15:25,130
[SPEAKER_01]: Those things happen in the world.

137
00:15:25,190 --> 00:15:36,002
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can focus on all the positive things in the world, all the lovely things, the adventures you can have, the excitement, the new discoveries, and so on, but

138
00:15:37,078 --> 00:15:42,743
[SPEAKER_01]: If you just see that part of the world, you are narrow-minded in that sense.

139
00:15:43,163 --> 00:15:50,209
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, when the other part affects you, you are going to be, you know, surprised in a horrific kind of way.

140
00:15:51,410 --> 00:15:53,432
[SPEAKER_00]: You're going to be... Well, that's the thing.

141
00:15:53,452 --> 00:16:00,518
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, a person can also then choose to just be focused on the negative and equally lose perspective.

142
00:16:02,119 --> 00:16:10,981
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is to find the middle ground and then you will feel responsibility on account of it and the weight of your actions and you will choose much more wisely.

143
00:16:11,061 --> 00:16:16,402
[SPEAKER_00]: Even, as I said, even without needing to renounce the whole world and become a monk and so on.

144
00:16:17,382 --> 00:16:26,504
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you basically, through your own reflection, see that, yes, you kind of don't like to think about, you know, what-ifs of the negative kind.

145
00:16:26,584 --> 00:16:28,164
[SPEAKER_00]: You only like to focus on the positive.

146
00:16:28,525 --> 00:16:30,705
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's why you should think about the negatives.

147
00:16:31,838 --> 00:16:32,418
[SPEAKER_00]: And what ifs?

148
00:16:34,379 --> 00:16:38,040
[SPEAKER_00]: Not, oh, I must, like, you know, get rid of my children and everything else.

149
00:16:38,160 --> 00:16:47,003
[SPEAKER_00]: No, but I must broaden the context and prevent that narrowing of my mind to occur on account of being absorbed and taking this pleasure for granted.

150
00:16:48,043 --> 00:16:59,807
[SPEAKER_00]: And equally, if all you're doing is just seeing the negative and thinking about the murders and accidents and so on, you could equally lose perspective then and recognize, yeah, but that's not happening all the time.

151
00:17:01,645 --> 00:17:03,868
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not that life is all about that either.

152
00:17:04,749 --> 00:17:11,316
[SPEAKER_00]: So, from that point of view, you could then, kind of, you know, broaden that narrowness by actually thinking on the good side.

153
00:17:11,476 --> 00:17:17,403
[SPEAKER_00]: On the, you know, there is friendship in the world, there is kindness, there is wholesome, there is virtue, there are results of good actions.

154
00:17:18,865 --> 00:17:20,546
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, basically, keep the balance.

155
00:17:21,870 --> 00:17:34,595
[SPEAKER_00]: Learn how to stay in the middle, regardless of what your tendency is, whether to just focus on the positive and ignore the negative, or just to dwell and wallow in negative, and just dismiss and be cynical in regard to any positive.

156
00:17:35,957 --> 00:17:49,423
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you are actually sustaining that balance, then, yes, as I said, your actions will feel differently, the weight behind them will feel differently, and your joy on account of children, or misery on account of world, will be something you will feel responsibly.

157
00:17:49,803 --> 00:17:52,224
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, you will know you're responsible for that state of mind.

158
00:17:53,424 --> 00:18:02,208
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you won't be projecting the fault onto the world, nor would you be projecting that all the happiness just comes from the world, and all I need to do is protect it.

159
00:18:02,771 --> 00:18:08,056
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you would see that it comes from your state of mind, where it chooses, basically, and how it chooses to attend things.

160
00:18:12,901 --> 00:18:24,952
[SPEAKER_01]: And, and I was, I was, I was thinking about, you know, that, so, you have people who just go with their emotions, no restraint.

161
00:18:26,788 --> 00:18:27,509
[SPEAKER_01]: No perspective.

162
00:18:28,389 --> 00:18:41,238
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you have also this kind of emotion of indifference, you know, crushing your feelings, so to speak, keeping them locked away.

163
00:18:41,878 --> 00:18:47,943
[SPEAKER_01]: You feel them, but you you keep them subdued, you know, like a like a soldier or a warrior.

164
00:18:48,804 --> 00:18:57,351
[SPEAKER_01]: The stoic indifference, stoic indifference, you know, managing your emotions, not letting them get out of hand, so overpowering them.

165
00:18:58,111 --> 00:19:11,815
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you are, you are experiencing pleasure, you're experiencing a heightened, you know, state of emotion, but you control yourself, you restrain yourself, and you just get the job done.

166
00:19:12,055 --> 00:19:14,396
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever you want, you need to do in your life, you just get it done.

167
00:19:14,856 --> 00:19:15,416
[SPEAKER_01]: You, eh...

168
00:19:18,827 --> 00:19:20,128
[SPEAKER_01]: You overpower your emotions.

169
00:19:20,148 --> 00:19:21,449
[SPEAKER_01]: You kind of ignore them in a way.

170
00:19:22,329 --> 00:19:24,271
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you know, which is strange.

171
00:19:24,331 --> 00:19:26,813
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't... you can't really ignore them.

172
00:19:27,533 --> 00:19:29,214
[SPEAKER_01]: To ignore them, you need to know that they are there.

173
00:19:30,235 --> 00:19:31,856
[SPEAKER_01]: And so you're kind of suppressing them.

174
00:19:31,876 --> 00:19:32,637
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I want to say.

175
00:19:32,917 --> 00:19:33,978
[SPEAKER_01]: You're kind of suppressing them.

176
00:19:35,118 --> 00:19:45,826
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you get this person who's like the stoic, indifferent soldier, warrior, you know, shoot some, kill people, and don't... try not to feel what that feels like.

177
00:19:47,761 --> 00:19:49,483
[SPEAKER_00]: What the original stoicism was about.

178
00:19:49,583 --> 00:19:52,065
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, I think people might think that's what it is.

179
00:19:52,385 --> 00:19:56,369
[SPEAKER_00]: Just to depress your feelings and sort of... Well, that's the other way.

180
00:19:56,389 --> 00:19:58,610
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just trying to deny the reality.

181
00:19:59,071 --> 00:20:01,213
[SPEAKER_00]: The existence of the fact that you are still feeling.

182
00:20:01,373 --> 00:20:01,713
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not...

183
00:20:02,394 --> 00:20:11,261
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're not being genuinely unmoved by feeling because you understood the unknown ability on account of your virtues and skillful behavior developed beforehand.

184
00:20:11,962 --> 00:20:21,973
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you're just trying to cover up the fact that you are still liable, but you're just trying to take even further control and basically kind of cement it all with your attitude, no, I don't feel anything.

185
00:20:22,233 --> 00:20:24,055
[SPEAKER_00]: But that cannot be sustained either.

186
00:20:24,075 --> 00:20:29,021
[SPEAKER_00]: See, that attitude is also dependent on the same things that your feelings and your joys depend on.

187
00:20:30,022 --> 00:20:34,805
[SPEAKER_00]: It depends on the circumstances, it depends on your determination, it depends on your intention, your health.

188
00:20:35,365 --> 00:20:42,709
[SPEAKER_00]: It depends on the current set of circumstances that are not too challenging, so that you can sustain the determination to not feel anything and overpower everything else.

189
00:20:43,430 --> 00:20:52,335
[SPEAKER_00]: But when things change, eh... again, when the health goes, senses start to fail, you start being overwhelmed from all sides, even that intention will go.

190
00:20:52,835 --> 00:20:57,318
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it has a limited... it has a basically, eh... it has its own shelf life.

191
00:21:00,178 --> 00:21:01,599
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

192
00:21:02,020 --> 00:21:08,025
[SPEAKER_01]: So, again, there is the gratification in emotions.

193
00:21:08,065 --> 00:21:09,966
[SPEAKER_01]: They are pleasant emotions, you know.

194
00:21:10,887 --> 00:21:22,237
[SPEAKER_01]: There is the danger of emotions, you know, being... you're just moved by them, you're making decisions upon them, whatever you feel, that's how you act, and so on.

195
00:21:22,277 --> 00:21:23,238
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's that danger.

196
00:21:24,879 --> 00:21:27,921
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how not to be moved by emotions?

197
00:21:27,962 --> 00:21:28,162
[SPEAKER_01]: How

198
00:21:31,038 --> 00:21:45,403
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you will be able to not be moved by emotions, which are on the level of your mind, if you have sufficiently practiced not being moved by all the things that are kind of the results of it.

199
00:21:46,444 --> 00:21:52,666
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you stopped acting carelessly, without perspective, unwholesomely with your body,

200
00:21:54,302 --> 00:22:01,345
[SPEAKER_00]: And sustain that as your day-to-day living, that perspective of certain non-action, you know, the aggregate of virtue we spoke about.

201
00:22:02,265 --> 00:22:11,909
[SPEAKER_00]: Equally, if you... if you have ceased all the unwholesome actions, actions by speech, they were rooted in... in all these other, like, subtle movements due to emotions and so on.

202
00:22:12,829 --> 00:22:20,072
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, eventually, you'll be able to equally remain unmoved and not act out of the mental state of... of whatever you're currently feeling.

203
00:22:20,732 --> 00:22:22,573
[SPEAKER_00]: But you cannot live your life

204
00:22:23,959 --> 00:22:35,284
[SPEAKER_00]: The way it is, which is undermined, life undermined and controlled, but influenced by emotions, and somehow magically expect to just be able to pull the emotion part out, without everything else changing.

205
00:22:35,364 --> 00:22:47,030
[SPEAKER_00]: That's, that's like putting, pulling the bottom, the, you know, like a house of cards, pulling the bottom card out, and expecting the the whole kind of creation to remain standing.

206
00:22:47,350 --> 00:22:48,190
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not possible.

207
00:22:50,291 --> 00:22:52,352
[SPEAKER_00]: Because everything is rooted in how you feel.

208
00:22:55,451 --> 00:22:58,873
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it's not like I must stop doing everything, because that's another doing.

209
00:22:59,173 --> 00:23:00,814
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, it's impossible to accomplish.

210
00:23:00,934 --> 00:23:02,454
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop not doing of everything.

211
00:23:03,475 --> 00:23:13,359
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can direct your doing in the direction that will tame your mind, reduce the dependence of you on your emotions and pleasures.

212
00:23:13,980 --> 00:23:19,682
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not about killing the horse and strangling it while you're riding it, because you'll come crashing with it.

213
00:23:20,283 --> 00:23:21,503
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about training it.

214
00:23:21,803 --> 00:23:24,765
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about not going where you know you shouldn't be going,

215
00:23:25,305 --> 00:23:29,207
[SPEAKER_00]: And developing that strength and ability of sense restraint and then sustaining it.

216
00:23:30,308 --> 00:23:39,112
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's... it's... there is no other, like... People might assume, oh, there's some... some great magical revelation will come out of my sense restraint.

217
00:23:39,212 --> 00:23:39,372
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

218
00:23:39,733 --> 00:23:42,074
[SPEAKER_00]: Sense restraint will come out of the sense restraint.

219
00:23:43,143 --> 00:23:48,205
[SPEAKER_00]: And once you start seeing that, you see a direct link then to how you develop and train that horse.

220
00:23:48,285 --> 00:23:48,385
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah!

221
00:23:48,885 --> 00:23:49,325
[SPEAKER_00]: Like this.

222
00:23:50,065 --> 00:23:56,327
[SPEAKER_00]: By preventing it from doing things it shouldn't be doing, by making it do things that it doesn't want to do, but we both know are good for it.

223
00:23:57,347 --> 00:24:05,390
[SPEAKER_00]: So, basically, again, skillful reflection, skillful behavior, precepts, and sustaining mindfulness on account of those actions throughout the day.

224
00:24:05,970 --> 00:24:07,190
[SPEAKER_00]: That is what you should do.

225
00:24:08,071 --> 00:24:10,851
[SPEAKER_00]: And you will know that that in itself is already a benefit.

226
00:24:11,452 --> 00:24:12,632
[SPEAKER_00]: That is already a reward.

227
00:24:15,070 --> 00:24:19,252
[SPEAKER_00]: And when the mind settles, when the horse settles into that, then you get even a higher reward.

228
00:24:20,252 --> 00:24:24,494
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, you are the one riding and controlling the horse and telling it where to go.

229
00:24:26,275 --> 00:24:35,859
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot drag you into the thorny bushes where you're gonna get strangled and, you know, with your head knocked off and so on, because you are in charge where it goes and it listens to you now.

230
00:24:36,799 --> 00:24:38,780
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is true, pleasant abiding here and now.

231
00:24:40,801 --> 00:24:41,941
[SPEAKER_00]: And that takes to be developed.

232
00:24:45,662 --> 00:24:55,185
[SPEAKER_01]: There is there is a state of mind that can be developed, that is, that can last, you can say, last forever, for as long as you're alive.

233
00:24:56,865 --> 00:24:59,206
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, that lasts as long as you're alive, not forever.

234
00:24:59,346 --> 00:24:59,566
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

235
00:25:00,126 --> 00:25:04,268
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and that is what you, in a way, that's what you're talking about, that state of peace.

236
00:25:04,848 --> 00:25:05,268
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

237
00:25:05,328 --> 00:25:06,608
[SPEAKER_00]: A state of wholesome peace.

238
00:25:07,389 --> 00:25:10,810
[SPEAKER_00]: And, obviously, you know, as I said, depending on how much

239
00:25:13,080 --> 00:25:16,782
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it depends how much you allow that horse to still engage with its wild desires.

240
00:25:17,282 --> 00:25:20,484
[SPEAKER_00]: To that extent, you can expect kind of the corresponding peace.

241
00:25:21,144 --> 00:25:24,566
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you don't allow it as much, you can have a greater peace.

242
00:25:24,826 --> 00:25:30,188
[SPEAKER_00]: If you still let it run wild every now and then, well, to that extent, you are diminishing your possibility of peace.

243
00:25:30,328 --> 00:25:36,912
[SPEAKER_00]: But, still, any effort you make towards taming, it will result to that degree of peace.

244
00:25:37,092 --> 00:25:39,713
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's never gonna go to waste.

245
00:25:41,221 --> 00:25:43,222
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that is ultimately what everybody wants.

246
00:25:43,822 --> 00:25:53,405
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, the reason people become infatuated with the, you know, pleasant experiences of the horse riding in the simile, is because of the peaceful pleasures that they get from it.

247
00:25:53,626 --> 00:25:55,886
[SPEAKER_00]: The satisfying, the gratification.

248
00:25:56,386 --> 00:26:02,929
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, gratification results in peace, results in temporary calmness of subsiding of your desires.

249
00:26:03,609 --> 00:26:06,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, you arrived at them through violent scratching of the itch.

250
00:26:07,070 --> 00:26:09,671
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's going to come back even worse because of that.

251
00:26:10,311 --> 00:26:17,513
[SPEAKER_00]: But, fundamentally, even pursuit of the desires and the pleasures and sensuality is done with the peace as the ultimate result of it.

252
00:26:18,153 --> 00:26:24,595
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if you don't want to be sense-restrained, if you don't want peace of this kind, you actually still want peace.

253
00:26:25,656 --> 00:26:26,636
[SPEAKER_00]: If you look deep down,

254
00:26:27,565 --> 00:26:37,188
[SPEAKER_00]: The end project of all the, you know, desires and gains and possessions and joys and emotions that people have, is that peaceful pleasure.

255
00:26:37,968 --> 00:26:38,388
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, nice.

256
00:26:38,808 --> 00:26:47,171
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why also people would then immediately jump into conclusion, into assumption that, oh, wow, this is so nice, and immediately assume that it will last forever.

257
00:26:48,231 --> 00:26:50,452
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's just too nice to think that it won't.

258
00:26:52,170 --> 00:26:59,394
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that whole notion of eternal and permanent is not some, like a, you know, a philosophical glitch in your mind.

259
00:26:59,434 --> 00:27:01,935
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's the result of your emotional dependence on pleasure.

260
00:27:02,416 --> 00:27:09,780
[SPEAKER_00]: You naturally assume that this will never change, because just thinking that it might change undermines that peace of that pleasure.

261
00:27:10,740 --> 00:27:13,642
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whether you admit it to yourself or not, you want peace.

262
00:27:14,362 --> 00:27:17,523
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just what you do in order to get it.

263
00:27:17,723 --> 00:27:25,987
[SPEAKER_00]: It's how you choose to go about it, that's what's going to determine whether you're going to get it or not, and whether you're going to pay a price for it or not.

264
00:27:26,107 --> 00:27:39,694
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when I... that kind of peace, actual peace, you know, being unmoved by a pleasant feeling, being unmoved by unpleasant feeling,

265
00:27:43,586 --> 00:27:57,592
[SPEAKER_01]: That that kind of... the peace to that extent, that you're still actually having pleasant feelings and unpleasant feelings, but you're unmoved by them, that peace is kind of horrifying for people.

266
00:27:58,172 --> 00:28:03,334
[SPEAKER_01]: When I explain to them, that kind of peace is absolutely horrifying.

267
00:28:03,554 --> 00:28:08,296
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like... That's because even the peace that they know, they have no understanding of it.

268
00:28:10,058 --> 00:28:23,550
[SPEAKER_00]: If they were to actually develop at least some basic wisdom in regard to their own situation, without, you know, giving things up and so on and enduring, they would... they would... well, it would be easy for them to come... come to terms with it.

269
00:28:23,750 --> 00:28:34,880
[SPEAKER_00]: But, eh... they're so unaware that any notion of... of that kind of stilling and, eh... and what you will be describing as peaceful, it just feels like, oh, that means I'm dead.

270
00:28:38,610 --> 00:28:39,092
[SPEAKER_00]: It is death.

271
00:28:39,192 --> 00:28:42,643
[SPEAKER_00]: That's people in the suttas who were scared when the Buddha would describe nibbāna.

272
00:28:45,312 --> 00:28:50,994
[SPEAKER_01]: What else is there, other than, you know, if you're not feeling something, you're dead.

273
00:28:51,294 --> 00:28:54,315
[SPEAKER_01]: If you'd... or you, you know, you're alive, but you're kind of...

274
00:28:54,395 --> 00:29:00,216
[SPEAKER_00]: It means they're so unaware, even of the nature of their feelings, that they don't even see that they often don't feel anything.

275
00:29:00,856 --> 00:29:01,777
[SPEAKER_00]: And yet they're not dead.

276
00:29:02,517 --> 00:29:03,197
[SPEAKER_00]: They just don't see it.

277
00:29:03,237 --> 00:29:03,977
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what it would have meant.

278
00:29:04,377 --> 00:29:07,338
[SPEAKER_00]: The neutral feeling is completely unseen, not understood.

279
00:29:07,678 --> 00:29:09,259
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it's felt unpleasantly.

280
00:29:11,099 --> 00:29:14,080
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you see it and understand it, it will actually feel pleasantly.

281
00:29:15,540 --> 00:29:15,940
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah.

282
00:29:17,181 --> 00:29:19,103
[SPEAKER_00]: The only reason they can even...

283
00:29:19,363 --> 00:29:26,908
[SPEAKER_00]: The only reason they can even be scared or horrified by the idea of peace is because deep down they recognize what that neutral feeling is.

284
00:29:28,069 --> 00:29:29,010
[SPEAKER_00]: It means they know it.

285
00:29:29,270 --> 00:29:34,954
[SPEAKER_00]: They just refuse to admit it to themselves.

286
00:29:34,974 --> 00:29:36,796
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of their dependence on that pleasure.

287
00:29:37,056 --> 00:29:37,977
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, exactly.

288
00:29:38,157 --> 00:29:42,740
[SPEAKER_00]: The pleasure, the dependence on that kind of movement, activity, happening, engagement.

289
00:29:44,441 --> 00:29:45,262
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the only way.

290
00:29:52,922 --> 00:29:56,644
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's what I said, you know, like, it's kind of relative.

291
00:29:56,664 --> 00:30:04,370
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't start as soon as you're born by that high dependence on a pleasant activity and engagement and so on.

292
00:30:04,750 --> 00:30:06,131
[SPEAKER_00]: You develop it carelessly.

293
00:30:07,262 --> 00:30:26,075
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why people who are super hyperactive, always engaged, even when they're slightly less engaged, still very much engaged, but slightly less engaged, mind will be filled with boredom and dread, and because not enough is happening, because their minds became even more dependent on even high level of activity.

294
00:30:26,095 --> 00:30:26,996
[SPEAKER_00]: And there is no end to that.

295
00:30:27,056 --> 00:30:28,097
[SPEAKER_00]: It is like an addiction.

296
00:30:28,157 --> 00:30:29,998
[SPEAKER_00]: The more you take, the more you'll need to take.

297
00:30:35,467 --> 00:30:44,692
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, not getting emotional is a sign that, you know, you are handling your feelings.

298
00:30:47,634 --> 00:30:50,135
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, hmm, it depends, I suppose.

299
00:30:50,155 --> 00:30:57,940
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, I know what you mean, but if somebody hears it like that, it means, okay, so I must just prevent emotions from happening and that's how I'm handling my feelings, no?

300
00:31:00,742 --> 00:31:03,746
[SPEAKER_00]: You must not be acting out of your emotions.

301
00:31:04,166 --> 00:31:05,648
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's how you're handling your feelings.

302
00:31:06,449 --> 00:31:11,174
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're able to let them arise to the extent they want to arise.

303
00:31:11,975 --> 00:31:13,517
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, you won't be moved by it.

304
00:31:13,837 --> 00:31:15,179
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you're handling your feelings.

305
00:31:20,196 --> 00:31:26,482
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yeah, managing your feelings, managing your emotions, is still management.

306
00:31:27,443 --> 00:31:30,025
[SPEAKER_01]: You're still having to deal... Well, you're still acting out of it.

307
00:31:30,105 --> 00:31:34,769
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you're overwhelmed by an emotion, thus you are needing to handle it.

308
00:31:36,831 --> 00:31:37,031
Yeah.

309
00:31:37,492 --> 00:31:39,934
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever you're feeling, whatever emotions are pressuring you,

310
00:31:40,871 --> 00:31:43,372
[SPEAKER_00]: You have the threshold of the virtue of the precepts.

311
00:31:43,532 --> 00:31:44,473
[SPEAKER_00]: That's your container.

312
00:31:44,613 --> 00:31:52,897
[SPEAKER_00]: Within that, you focus on not losing perspective in a situation for as long as the emotion lasts, and not giving in to the pressure to act out of it.

313
00:31:55,319 --> 00:31:57,200
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not going to be perfect in the beginning.

314
00:31:57,240 --> 00:32:02,623
[SPEAKER_00]: You will be still doing it, to some extent, out of desire to manage it, get rid of it.

315
00:32:03,143 --> 00:32:05,084
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if these, sort of, eh...

316
00:32:06,386 --> 00:32:12,887
[SPEAKER_00]: frameworks are protected, that motivation will subside and you'll start doing it more and more rightly.

317
00:32:16,928 --> 00:32:33,011
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's like that, as you're saying before, so you handle your emotions, don't act out of them, you know, you can then gain a restraint and so on, you gain a perspective on your feelings,

318
00:32:34,938 --> 00:32:43,004
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you can start understanding their nature, what the fundamental nature of feelings are, and thus be free from them.

319
00:32:43,024 --> 00:32:48,829
[SPEAKER_01]: So when, no matter how intense they come, manifest... Yeah, yeah.

320
00:32:48,849 --> 00:32:54,673
[SPEAKER_00]: At that point, you would be able to start seeing that freedom from feeling is not freedom from

321
00:32:57,483 --> 00:33:02,786
[SPEAKER_00]: Feelings arising, it's freedom from basically, eh... you owning them.

322
00:33:03,026 --> 00:33:05,307
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot own them now, even if you want to.

323
00:33:06,628 --> 00:33:11,490
[SPEAKER_00]: They're truly seen as impersonal, as impersonal things out there in the world that come and go.

324
00:33:12,231 --> 00:33:14,912
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they cannot move you, because it's not yours, not your problem.

325
00:33:15,332 --> 00:33:16,753
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's truly not felt.

